course: everyday
lesson: 24
title: Bees, wasps and stings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Bees and wasps sting to protect themselves and their nest, not to hunt people, so they rarely sting if they are left alone.
explain: A honeybee's sting has tiny hooks like a fish-hook; it gets stuck in our skin and tears away from the bee, so the bee dies, while a wasp's smooth sting can be used again and again.
explain: If a bee or wasp buzzes near you, stay calm and walk slowly away: swatting and flapping makes it feel attacked and more likely to sting.
explain: After a bee sting, scrape the stinger out quickly with a fingernail or a card, wash the place and hold something cold on it to ease the pain and swelling.
explain: For most people a sting is just painful, but some people are allergic: if someone's face, lips or throat swell, or they find it hard to breathe or feel faint, call the emergency number at once and use their adrenaline pen if they have one.
explain: For example, wasps love sweet drinks at picnics, so a wasp can crawl into an open can and sting a child's mouth: always look before you sip.
explain: A boy in Kansas was stung by a bee and his lips and throat began to swell; his mum used his allergy pen and called 911, and doctors said acting fast saved him.
explain: A girl in Kent swatted at a wasp nest with a stick; dozens of wasps came out and stung her many times before she got indoors.
fact: Bees and wasps sting mainly to defend themselves and their nest.
fact: A honeybee leaves its barbed stinger in the skin and dies after stinging.
fact: A wasp can sting more than once.
fact: Swatting at a bee or wasp makes a sting more likely.
fact: A bee stinger should be scraped out quickly, because it keeps pumping venom for a short while.
fact: A cold pack helps reduce the pain and swelling of a sting.
fact: Swelling of the face or throat, trouble breathing or feeling faint after a sting is an emergency.
fact: People with a serious sting allergy may carry an adrenaline pen, such as an EpiPen, given into the outer thigh.
fact: A sting inside the mouth or throat is dangerous because the swelling can block breathing.
fact: Wasps are attracted to sweet food and drinks.
fact: Never poke, throw things at or try to knock down a bees' or wasps' nest.
fact: Bees are very important because they carry pollen between flowers and help plants make fruit and seeds.
fact: Some people have a severe allergy to stings that can make their throat swell and needs emergency help at once.
fact: Disturbing a nest makes wasps or bees attack in large numbers.
exercise: Why do bees and wasps usually sting? | To defend themselves and their nest | To catch people as food | Because they enjoy stinging | To lay their eggs under your skin to hatch
exercise: A wasp is buzzing around your head. What is the best thing to do? | Stay calm and walk slowly away | Swat it hard with your hand straight away | Flap your arms and scream | Blow on it to push it off
exercise: Why does a honeybee die after stinging a person? | Its hooked stinger gets stuck and tears away from its body | It runs out of breath while stinging and cannot fly back | Human blood is poison to bees | It is too tired to fly home
exercise: Which insect can sting you more than once? | A wasp | A honeybee | A butterfly | A ladybird
exercise: Why should you remove a bee stinger quickly? | It keeps pumping venom for a short while | It will grow deeper roots into your skin | It turns into a new bee | It makes the skin turn blue
exercise: What helps the pain and swelling of a sting? | Holding something cold on it | Rubbing it hard | Covering it with sand | Scratching it until it bleeds
exercise: After a sting, a girl's lips swell and she struggles to breathe. What should happen? | Call the emergency number at once | Put a plaster on the sting and wait | Let her sleep it off | Give her a sweet drink to calm her
exercise: What is an EpiPen? | A pen of adrenaline for people with serious allergies | A pen for writing down where and when you were stung | A spray that keeps bees away | A plaster for covering stings
exercise: Why is a sting inside the mouth so dangerous? | Swelling there can block breathing | The mouth has no feeling at all | Stings in the mouth hurt less | Saliva makes the venom much stronger
exercise: Why should you look into your can of juice at a picnic? | A wasp may have crawled in for the sugar | The sun may have spoiled the juice in the can | Fizzy drinks lose their bubbles | Cans are often not quite full
exercise: You find a wasps' nest in the garden shed. What should you do? | Keep away and tell an adult | Knock it down with a stick | Throw stones at it | Spray it with the hose
exercise: Why are bees so important for us? | They carry pollen and help plants make fruit | They eat all the flies in summer | They clean the air we breathe | They keep wasps away from gardens and flower beds
exercise: How should a bee stinger be removed? | Scraped out with a fingernail or card | Pushed in deeper with a finger | Left in for a few days | Pulled out by squeezing it between finger and thumb
exercise: Most people who get stung by a bee… | have pain and swelling that goes away | always have to go to hospital | never feel anything at all | become allergic for the rest of their lives
test: Oliver in Kent sees a bee land on his arm. What is the wisest move? | Keep still and calm and let it fly off | Slap it quickly before it gets a chance to sting | Shake his arm wildly | Blow hard on it
test: Ruby is stung by a wasp in Melbourne. There is no stinger in her skin. Why not? | A wasp's sting is smooth and stays with the wasp | The stinger has already dissolved in her skin | Wasps never actually sting | She must have been bitten instead
test: Which is true? | A honeybee can sting only once | A honeybee can sting many times | A wasp dies after every sting | Bees sting to hunt for food
test: A boy in Florida is stung and soon his face swells and he feels faint. He has an EpiPen. What should happen? | An adult uses the EpiPen and calls 911 | He waits an hour to see if it passes | He drinks lots of water and lies down | Someone rubs mud on the sting
test: Why do wasps often come to picnics in late summer? | They are attracted to sweet food and drinks | They like the colour of the picnic blankets | They are hunting for people | They want to rest in the shade under the table
test: What would happen if you hit a wasps' nest with a stick? | Many wasps would come out to defend it and sting | The wasps would all fly away for good and never come back | The nest would turn into honey | Nothing, wasps cannot sense it
test: A girl is stung on the hand. She has no allergy. What is the right first aid? | Remove any stinger, wash it and hold something cold on it | Wrap it tightly in a bandage | Put it in very hot water | Squeeze the sting hard to push venom out
test: Why is it a bad idea to wave your arms at a bee? | It feels attacked and is more likely to sting | Bees like waving and come closer to play | Waving makes bees lose their sting | Bees can only see moving arms, so it will find you
test: Which sign after a sting means an emergency? | Trouble breathing | A small red bump | A little itch | Mild pain for an hour
test: wasp buzzing my lolly, swat it?? | No, stay calm and move away slowly | Yes, swat it hard | Yes, wave your arms | Throw your drink at it
test: bee stung me, sting still in there | Scrape it out quickly | Leave it in for a day | Squeeze it hard | Pour juice on it
test: got stung, now his face is swelling n can't breathe | Emergency, call for help now | Wait a few hours | Give him a cold drink and wait | Just a cold pack is enough
test: wasp's nest in the shed, poke it down with a stick? | No, never poke a nest | Yes, quickly before they wake | Yes, throw stones at it | Yes, if you wear a hat
test: friend got stung and now her face is puffing up and she can't breathe properly | Call emergency services now and get an adult | Give her a sweet drink and wait to see if it passes | Put ice on and carry on playing | Tell her to lie down and sleep
test: found a wasp nest in the shed, gonna poke it | No, leave it and tell an adult | Yes, with a long stick | Yes, if you run fast after | Yes, if you wear a hoodie
test: Liam says one sting can never hurt anyone badly. true? | No, a sting can be deadly for someone with an allergy | Yes, one sting is always small | Yes, only big swarms are dangerous to people | Yes, unless it is a bee
test: did u hear Jayden got stung by like 50 wasps at the picnic cos he waved his arms at them | Real lesson: swatting makes stings more likely, so stay calm; «50» is probably exaggerated | Real lesson: waving arms scares wasps away | It proves picnics are the only place wasps live | It means wasps sting only boys
test: my mate says bees can sting you loads of times like wasps | Not true for honeybees; a honeybee leaves its stinger and dies, but a wasp can sting more than once | He is right, both sting many times | He is right, bees sting more than wasps | He is right, but only in summer
test: Hannah screamed and ran from a bee and fell over in front of everyone, shall i post it | No, she was scared and posting it would embarrass her | Yes, because screaming at a bee is silly and everyone should see | Yes, so others learn to be brave | Yes, but only on a private story
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the fate of a honeybee subsequent to stinging a person? | Its barbed stinger remains in the skin and the bee dies | It withdraws its stinger and stings again | It loses its wings but survives | It returns to the hive unharmed
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a bee's stinger ought to be removed promptly. | It continues to pump venom for a short period | It will grow deeper into the skin over many days | It attracts other bees to the place | It will fall out of its own accord at once
exam: It being given that swelling of the face or throat and difficulty in breathing follow a sting, select the most accurate characterisation of the situation. | It constitutes a medical emergency | It is an ordinary reaction requiring no attention | It may be treated by rest alone | It indicates only that the sting was painful
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the attraction of wasps. | Wasps are attracted to sweet food and drink | Wasps are attracted solely to the colour black | Wasps are repelled by sweet food and drink | Wasps are attracted only to running water
